Z||000000||montage||02-13-2001||09:12 AM||||This is the second post on a similar question about the hypothetical performance  with various axle ratios. Sorry for the length but I think I was misunderstood and this decreased response. I would like to know from the members if you had your choice what would be the ideal axial ratio to increase the performance of an 88 formula 5-sp (stock so far but runs strong) in 0-60, ¼ mile, and possibly autocross. I've done searches on the forum without luck on what axial ratio does to performance, maybe those with 4.10 ratio could comment on the benefits. I was hoping to get data from those with Dragstrip2000 to calculate the effect of axial ratio for 3.6, 3.7, 3.8, 3.9, 4, and 4.1 with stock tires (215/60/15) on the 0-60 times and ¼ mile. 88formula calculated the data for the 4.10 ratio and I really appreciate the information thanks!<BR> <BR>I see theogre's point but think he misunderstood my intentions and stated that one should have the axle ratio before someone does the calculations. My intent is not to change axle ratio but to adjust the effective axle ratio by changing tire size. The reason I would like the data so I can plot the performance versus axle ratio (keeping the tire size stock) to see if there is an optimum ratio for performance. I would then like to look at tires to see if I can find a size to give me that effective ratio.<BR> <BR>I currently am evaluating a tire size of 245/35/17 (rear) and 225/40/16 (front) which would give me an effective ratio (using stock tires) of 3.81 so performance information would be important. This would do several things, first tires are one of the most important aspects of handling and these tires would provide a lower profile and wider width, second it would lower the car 0.7 inches while retaining the stock suspension geometry (travel, springs, etc), and finally possible increase the 0-60 and ¼ mile performance. I would like member's comments on this idea and I should stress that my priority is performance/cost over looks. Finally, if I get the axle ratio and performance data and decide on the specified tires mentioned for purchase ( I am leaning that way but would like the data first) I would like wheel size suggestions. Thanks for you help in advance all should be proud this is a great forum. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||02-13-2001||11:20 AM||||The simple idea is:<P>smaller circumfrence = more torque/faster acceleration<P>larger circumfrence = lower torque/faster high speed<P>small tires will give you more torque to the ground, but you will have to rev higher to get the same speed.<P>large tires will give weaker performance off the line, but you'll get better highway milage and a higher top end.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||02-13-2001||11:46 AM||||A friend of mine had a 4.10 put in his 86 GT , the car has an ocelot exhaust , no cat converter , MSD , hypertech chip and 16" wheels. He HATES IT .. the car tops out on the highway , his 1/4 mile times got worse as did his 0 to 60. IT moved the engine right out of it's powerband..<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
